  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.74,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["U10", "J2", "J3"],
      "pins": ["U10.6", "U10.7", "J2.2", "J2.3", "J3.2", "J3.3"],
      "nets": ["CAN_L_P", "CAN_L_N", "CAN1_P", "CAN1_N"],
      "observed": "On the CAN BUS sheet, the second isolated CAN transceiver U10 (driven by MCP2515 U9) outputs its bus lines to nets labelled 'CAN_L_P' (pin 7) and 'CAN_L_N' (pin 6) — note the letter 'L'. The two M12 CAN connectors J2 ('CAN Interface A') and J3 ('CAN Interface B') instead bring out pins 2/3 as 'CAN1_P'/'CAN1_N' (digit '1'). Because the net names differ, U10's CAN bus never reaches either connector, and the connectors' CAN1_P/CAN1_N pins are undriven (and also miss the D9/D10 TVS protection, which the spec places on CAN1_P/N).",
      "expected": "Per the spec, the board implements 'two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 ... broken out on M12 circular connectors', with TVS arrays D9/D10 on 'CAN0_P/N, CAN1_P/N'. The CAN1 transceiver's output nets must share the same name as the connector pins (CAN1_P/CAN1_N) so that U9/U10 actually drive the second CAN bus out to the connectors.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U10 output pins labelled 'CAN_L_P' (pin 7) and 'CAN_L_N' (pin 6); J2/J3 pins 2/3 labelled 'CAN1_P'/'CAN1_N' (CAN BUS sheet, tile showing the CAN Interface A/B connectors)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) ... broken out on M12 circular connectors J2 ('CAN Interface A') and J13 ('CAN Interface B')"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "D9, D10 — CDSOT23-SM712 TVS diode arrays on CAN0_P/N, CAN1_P/N"}
      ],
      "impact": "The second CAN bus (CAN1, from U9/U10) is electrically disconnected from the external M12 connectors: the connectors' CAN1 pins float and the transceiver's CAN_L lines go nowhere, so any Odrive/actuator on the CAN1 bus cannot communicate, and that bus also loses its SM712 transient protection.",
      "confidence": 0.74
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic is largely consistent with the spec (16 MHz crystals on Y1/Y2, LTC4412+P-MOS ideal diode U6/Q1, SN74LVC1G07 e-stop sinks, WS2812B chain, GLOBAL_EN on SW1), but the second CAN transceiver U10 drives nets named 'CAN_L_P/CAN_L_N' while the CAN connectors expect 'CAN1_P/CAN1_N' — a letter-vs-digit net-name mismatch that leaves the CAN1 bus unconnected to its connectors and unprotected."
}

